# =============================================================
# instance/config/connectors.yml — what this cabinet may READ, and how
# =============================================================
# EXAMPLE / TEMPLATE. Copy to instance/config/connectors.yml and describe YOUR
# systems. Absent file = no connectors = the cabinet reads nothing outward and
# says so; nothing here is built in, and the framework knows no vendor, tool,
# industry or entity kind. That is the point: a connector is three things — a
# CREDENTIAL, an INVENTORY CALL, and a TIMESTAMP FIELD — and whatever answers
# that shape is a connector, whether it holds boards, repositories, case files,
# patient lists, catalogues or shipments.
#
# TWO WAYS TO FILL THIS FILE. Hand-write an entry below, or let onboarding do it:
# the "Connect a tool" step offers a curated, browsable catalog drawn from
# instance/config/connector-templates.yml.example, and writing an entry there is
# the same as writing one here — it declares a credential env NAME and a
# read-only inventory call, and the entry it appends is exactly the shape below.
#
# WHAT THE CABINET DOES WITH IT. At onboarding it runs the `gather_connectors`
# action: every declared connector is read ONCE, read-only, and what comes back
# is names, dates, actors and counts — never bodies, never rows. Those names are
# ranked by what RECURS across your systems, and you are asked which one to open
# first. Depth is spent only where you point.
#
# TWO KINDS OF ENTRY LIVE HERE. Everything above describes the `inventory:` kind
# — a list of YOUR things. An entry may instead carry `search:`, which is a
# SEARCH TOOL: it holds nothing of yours, it is never swept, and it never counts
# as a connected source. What it does is let the cabinet run the look-ups the
# opening question already proposes — you say what you do, and it goes and finds
# out about the words you used. That sends YOUR SENTENCE to that provider, which
# is why connecting one is the consent for it and why the query is printed back
# on the card. The shape is documented in the search-template section of
# instance/config/connector-templates.yml.example; a search call is held to its
# own read-only ceiling, which refuses any address or body naming a write.
#
# WHO YOU ARE IS ASKED, NEVER READ OFF THE CREDENTIAL. A token issued to an
# integration answers "who am I" with a service account nobody sits at, so the
# `identity:` block below is used only for what the estate calls ITSELF. Which
# of the actors is YOU comes from you: after the sweep the cabinet lists the
# account identifiers each connector actually stamped on its rows and asks you
# to pick yours (`record_operator_identity`). Picked, it is matched WHOLE and
# EXACT — never by resemblance — and every claim about your work then carries
# its share ("3 of 56 here are yours, 53 carry another actor"). Skip a
# connector and it keeps saying it cannot tell, which is the honest answer.
#
# DECLARE `actor_field` OR NOTHING THERE CAN EVER BE YOURS. A connector with no
# actor path returns rows nobody is attached to, so even your own account
# attributes nothing in it — the cabinet says so per connector rather than
# leaving you to notice.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CEILING, which is not configurable and not negotiable
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * READ ONLY. `method: GET`, or `method: POST` whose body is a GraphQL READ
#     document. Every other verb is refused before a socket is opened, a POST
#     body that is not {query, variables, operationName} is refused, a document
#     containing a `mutation` or `subscription` token is refused, and a method-
#     override header is refused. There is no spelling of this file that can
#     make the cabinet write to your systems.
#   * HTTPS ONLY, AND NO REDIRECTS. Your credential goes to the host you named
#     or nowhere at all.
#   * BOUNDED. A whole sweep is a few dozen requests with a per-call timeout, a
#     response-size cap and an item cap.
#   * CREDENTIALS BY NAME. `credential_env` names an environment variable; the
#     VALUE is read at call time and never appears in a result, a log, an error
#     message or an artifact.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# IF SOMETHING COMES BACK EMPTY, READ THE REASON — it is never just "empty"
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# A first pass at this file is usually wrong somewhere, so every declared path
# reports whether it RESOLVED. `items_path_missed:<path>` means the list was not
# where you said (a real empty system says `inventory_returned_no_items`
# instead); `name_path_missed:<path>` means items came back and none carried a
# name there; and a date or actor path that resolved on NONE of the items is
# named in the disclosure rather than left to read as a system that has no
# clock and no people. Fix the path, re-run — the sweep is a glance, not an
# ingest, and it is meant to be re-run.
#
# TREAT THIS FILE AS PRIVILEGED. It says where a credential may be sent. Keep it
# owned by the captain (the same custody as instance/config/egress.yml), because
# an attacker who could edit it could point your token at their host.
#
# NOTE ON EGRESS. The sweep asks instance/config/egress.yml first. With the
# shipped default (`enforce: false`, allow-all) it proceeds. If you have turned
# enforcement on, add each connector's host to `allow_hosts` or the sweep will
# refuse it — visibly, with the reason, rather than as a wall of failures.
# =============================================================

schema: cabinet.connectors/v1

connectors:
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # A REST connector: one GET, a list of things, a timestamp on each.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # - name: code                     # YOUR label. It appears in the evidence
  #                                  # line you are shown; nothing reads it.
  #   credential_env: EXAMPLE_TOKEN  # env var NAME, never the value
  #   inventory:
  #     url: https://api.example.test/v3/things?per_page=100&page={page}
  #     method: GET
  #     auth_header: Authorization   # default: Authorization
  #     auth_format: "Bearer {credential}"   # default: "{credential}"
  #     headers:
  #       Accept: application/json
  #     items_path: ""               # dotted path to the list; "" = the root
  #     name_field: full_name        # dotted path, per item
  #     updated_field: updated_at    # dotted path, per item
  #     date_encoding: iso           # iso (default) | epoch_ms | epoch_s.
  #                                  # DECLARED, never guessed. A system that
  #                                  # stamps in epoch time is not an undated
  #                                  # system, but read as text it fails every
  #                                  # date test downstream and the sweep will
  #                                  # tell you it found no clock. An encoding
  #                                  # this cabinet does not know is refused by
  #                                  # name rather than read as ISO.
  #     actor_field: owner.login     # optional: who owns/created/is on the
  #                                  # thing. One path, or a LIST of paths —
  #                                  # and a path may resolve to a person, or
  #                                  # to a list of them (assignees, parties,
  #                                  # participants), which is usually where
  #                                  # YOU are. One name is read out of each
  #                                  # person, never their record.
  #     # actor_field:
  #     #   - owner.login
  #     #   - assignees
  #   page:
  #     start: 1
  #     max_pages: 3                 # {page} in the url/body is substituted
  #     size: 100                    # optional, and worth setting: the page size
  #                                  # you asked for. A SHORT last page then
  #                                  # proves the estate was exhausted, so the
  #                                  # "there may be more" caveat is printed only
  #                                  # when it is true. Omit it and the caveat is
  #                                  # printed on every complete sweep.
  #
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # A GraphQL connector: one POST carrying a READ document.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # - name: tracker
  #   credential_env: EXAMPLE_GRAPHQL_TOKEN
  #   inventory:
  #     url: https://api.example.test/graphql
  #     method: POST
  #     json:
  #       query: "query { things (limit: 100, page: {page}) { id name updated_at creator { name } } }"
  #     items_path: data.things
  #     name_field: name
  #     updated_field: updated_at
  #     actor_field: creator.name    # who made the thing. Declare SOMETHING here
  #                                  # or nothing in this connector can ever be
  #                                  # attributed to you (see the header). It
  #                                  # belongs beside the other per-item paths,
  #                                  # under `inventory:` — and the document has
  #                                  # to SELECT the field it names, or the path
  #                                  # resolves on nothing.
  #   page:
  #     start: 1
  #     max_pages: 6
  #   identity:                      # optional, and worth declaring:
  #     url: https://api.example.test/graphql   # the account/owner names the
  #     method: POST                            # ranker DEMOTES, so your own
  #     json:                                   # name does not win every rank
  #       query: "query { me { name } account { name } }"
  #     value_paths:                            # NOT who you are: a token can
  #       - data.me.name                        # belong to an integration, so
  #       - data.account.name                   # attribution is asked, not read
